\f1\fs22 Almost immediately and for days after he really went a cheeseburger, Tony descended into a fugue. Of course he missed Sarah. Sure, he\'92d released her through the catharsis song, and exhorted the Elvi to do likewise. And thank tax he had, because there would not be enough vitreous china in all the bathrooms in Ulyssia on which they could visit their rubble-producing rage. Truth be known, Ulyssia was a single song away from an Elvi uprising that, though it may be put down, would have fractured the uneasy compliance of all Cloudborne.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony missed more than Sarah; he missed her baby too. Something about the unborn Little Clinton that calmed and soothed him. He was almost his muse, Tony suspected, as if the lyrics that earned him the title 
\f2\i Great Teacher
\f1\i0  were more than inspired by him, they were dictated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course, that\'92s all nonsense, Tony realised. But in the days that followed, the absence of Sarah and bub deepened his depression. He refused to leave the ruins of the mansion, which would farther wedge open the gap to Sarah. He refused to eat, aside from the odd cheeseburger. He couldn\'92t sleep, partly because he spent nights under a dusty blanket, sitting knees-up, wedged into the one remaining solid corner of what was once his bedroom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the seventh day, the first High Elvis\'97of the recently convened \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Elvi Parliament of the Ghetto\'97visited Tony in his ruined lodgings, and having gently taken his elbow and reverently coaxed him to his feet, led him to a parliamentary Cadillac, in which Tony lay down on the back seat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cadillac, driven personally by the High Elvis, whisked Tony to new lodgings.\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 If Tony\'92s mind had in any way been present, if he\'92d found the impetus or curiosity to look out the window, he may have gazed upon the open market where, on the night of the Vegas Show, Kleo bought his jumpsuit of many colours, Solo his leathers, and their Chilo his army uniform.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cadillac pulled up less than two blocks from Graceland, where that fateful Vegas Show had been held all those months ago (well, a little less than three months ago). Two Elvi emerged from the ramshackle building typical in the heart of the Ghetto, and eased Tony from the back seat. Tony took a moment to find balance and strength in his legs. He looked up at the fa\'e7ade of the building which leant ominously over the footpath. Its dusty brickwork and gaunt windows sagged forward to such an extent, the breath of a hearty child might send the whole conglomeration tumbling into the street. Elvi assisted Tony through the doors. Expensive department store air conditioning smacked them in their faces a like chilled cheesecloth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Belying the third-world exterior of the building, the interior had been recently gutted and retrofitted as a very modern, very luxurious recording studio, with control rooms, a large studio that could fit an orchestra, a couple of smaller studios, vocal rooms for every mood, and a drum room for every kind of drummer which, of course, was precisely one. Drummers, for their part, remarked that the whole place had been designed by an attention-seeking front man. Or, possibly, a lead guitarist or saxophone player. In back, was an equally opulent two-bedroom flat, complete with full kitchen, dining, living, and media rooms. There was a fully stocked bar in its own room, and full-sized bathrooms off both upstairs bedrooms, one of which the Elvi took Tony and caste him on the bed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi had plans for the Great Teacher; they had great-ish expectations, reasonably based on past performance. They needed the Great Teacher to keep writing; there were still many great tunes out there begging for lyrics the Elvi could give up cracking skulls over.\
